+        // produce an incorrect match offset. So we defer to the slower (but
+        // always correct) capture-based implementation.
+        if haystack[cand.start() - 1] == b'\n' {
+            return Err(());
+        }
         let (_, slen) = bstr::decode_utf8(&haystack[cand]);
```

**Root cause:** `WordMatcher::fast_find` implements `--word-regexp` via the regex `(^|\W)(<pattern>)(\W|$)` and takes a fast path that strips one codepoint from each end of the overall match to recover the inner match. That assumption only holds when the ends came from `\W`. When a match begins at a line boundary inside the haystack, the leading alternation matches via the zero-width `^` anchor, so the first character belongs to the original pattern's match — stripping it truncated the match (`some.domain.com` → `ome.domain.com`). The fix defers such matches to the capture-based slow path, which is always correct.

